  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 220 PM EST Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and seasonably cool this weekend through \nthe middle of next week.\n\nAn upper low remains centered over the eastern Great Lakes this\nafternoon. Surface high pressure over central US is slowly \npushing towards the area over the next few days. Cold air \nadvection behind yesterday's cold front has pushed temperatures \nback to near normal with temperatures currently in the lower \n60s. High temperatures Sunday will be similar in the lower to \nmid 60s. There will be a slight warm up Mon thru Wed as high \npressure centers over the area with highs in the upper 60s to \nnear 70F. Dry weather continues with mostly sunny skies through \nat least mid week.\n\nLow temperatures late tonight/early Sunday morning will be in \nthe mid to upper 30s inland and lower/mid 40s along the coast. \nPatchy frost is possible, particularly in the Piedmont and \ncounties that have already ended their growing season, but there\ncould be some frost into interior portions of S-central VA and \nNE NC as well. Given dewpoints expected in the mid 30s, light NW\nwinds, and some cloud cover over the southern half of the area \novernight, have raised low temperatures up to 5 degrees across \nthe area. Based on model ensembles and hi-res guidance, the NBM \nlow temperatures are an outlier. With this, no frost advisories \nhave been issued for counties where the growing season \ncontinues.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The weather pattern potentially becomes more \namplified later next week with low-end rain chances returning \nfollowed by a brief surge of colder air.\n\nThe 12z/07 EPS, GEFS, and GEPS all continue to depict an \nanomalous 500mb trough developing across the southeast and Mid- \nAtlantic by Thurs-Fri of next week. Low-end rain chances return \nlater Thursday/Thursday night with the potential for developing \nlow pressure ahead of the deepening trough. Confidence in \ndetails at this forecast range is low, but the GEFS and EPS show\na 40-80% chance of at least 0.01\" of rain with the highest \nchances in the N and a 10-20% chance of at least 0.5\" of rain. A\nsurge of colder air follows with below average temperatures \nfavored by Friday-Saturday of next week. With this trend, there \nis a chance this could end the growing season for the remaining \nareas.",
